WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.700
So I've played Armored Core 6. And long&nbsp;
story short, the game is just good...
&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.961
I've never felt so privileged to show off a game before.

00:00:45.961 --> 00:00:50.404
I know I'm biased since I have this channel and everything - but the game is just so special:

00:00:50.404 --> 00:00:54.981
it marks the point where an old franchise is coming back to life. 

00:00:54.981 --> 00:00:59.513
It represents a&nbsp;  
genre that so many new people will get to try for the first time, 

00:00:59.513 --> 00:01:04.680
and yet it's still undeniably&nbsp;
a From Software game, with so many familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:09.720
hallmarks of their modern game design.
But, if you are a newcomer to the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.642
you might be thinking that this combat and UI is overwhelming. And you're right - it is.

00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:20.400
But trust me when I say that&nbsp;
it's worth taking the time to understand it all.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.640
So let's start at the beginning...with&nbsp;
the first mission of Armored Core 6.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:36.360
The game starts with you being sent&nbsp;
to infiltrate the planet "Rubicon 3".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.800
But... it doesn't exactly go according to plan.
&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.620
Clearly, the Rubiconians don't want&nbsp;
anyone entering their airspace.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:55.980
...and it wouldn't be a Fromsoft game if you&nbsp;
didn't start the game half dead already.
&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.400
The man speaking here is your Handler&nbsp;
"Walter". And you are his hound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.400
trained to follow his every command.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.700
With this, we're shot closer to&nbsp;
where we were supposed to be.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.720
This is the mech you start the&nbsp;
game with. It has an assault rifle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.540
a laser blade, and multi-lock missiles.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.200
In this next section, I'll try to break down how the game plays.

00:03:38.861 --> 00:03:43.140
What I'm doing now is called an&nbsp;
"Assault Boost". It's the most efficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:49.320
way to cover great distances.
It's also really, really good in combat..you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.120
can even dodge during it, like this.
If you come to a stop while grounded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.740
you will walk, like this - but gliding with your&nbsp;
boosters on is generally better.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:04.740
and this is your quick boost - your dodge.
Then there's one button to jump, and ascend.
&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:10.680
All of this requires energy, which&nbsp;
recovers really rapidly while you're grounded.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.340
This marks the first AC wreck that we've found.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.560
So there are multiple paths through this huge city...
...and it's swarming with enemies.
&nbsp;

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.880
Thanks to the dialogue in this game, missions are really&nbsp;
engaging. Either Walter's telling you something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:17.220
or you're eavesdropping on enemies, or your&nbsp;
foes are freaking out because they have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:23.000
fight you. It's fun.
&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.020
Let's talk missiles. By default, all missiles go to&nbsp;
a single target, like this.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:33.540
But if you hold down the fire button, you&nbsp;
can launch at multiple enemies, instead.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.180
You're going to be outnumbered a lot&nbsp;
in Armored Core, so staying mobile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.360
constantly firing off missiles&nbsp;
is just a good way to play.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.180
I overcome multiple enemies&nbsp;
here by firing missiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:02.160
and by making use of line of sight&nbsp;
to avoid enemies while I reload.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:42.420
There's one last AC wreck to find.
And this is the pilot whose legacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:50.280
you pillage, to obtain your new name.
Perhaps unsurprisingly, it's time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.920
prepare for a boss fight...so as you can see -&nbsp;
I've toggled on my target assist, shown here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:02.580
This is an automatic hard lock mode that's&nbsp;
great for focusing on a single enemy.
&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:16.680
It's your typical early Fromsoft boss -&nbsp;
an enemy that you probably won't be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:20.580
to defeat unless you're comfortable&nbsp;
with the fundamentals of the game.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.220
You need to always keep moving, to avoid enemy&nbsp;
fire. Get hit too many times, and your stability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:41.040
will break. Watch and&nbsp;
listen for warning signs. Try to stay close, and use your blade. Use the environment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:47.520
block enemy attacks. And while all this is going&nbsp;
on, you need to make sure you're using all four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.520
of your weapons efficiently, so that you can break&nbsp;
through enemy defenses, and deal massive damage.

00:08:32.353 --> 00:08:36.960
If you've played previous AC games...you&nbsp;
know how good it feels to hear that.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:43.380
After you've listened to all of your new messages,&nbsp;
you'll enter this; the garage. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:51.000
you can customise your AC. Sell its old parts.&nbsp;
Buy new parts...and inevitably realise that

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:57.540
the new parts are too heavy for your legs. So:&nbsp;
buy new legs. And on, and on it goes. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:04.500
get lost in a sea of complex cost benefit&nbsp;
analysis here - or you can just buy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:11.820
biggest gun you can carry, and remember that&nbsp;
red numbers bad. Blue numbers good..and get by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:17.460
that way, instead. Whatever your approach; it's&nbsp;
surprisingly fun, once you get the hang of it.
&nbsp;

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:25.200
But that's not all you can do in the garage. You&nbsp;
can also practice in the training simulator, earn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:29.807
upgrades in the arena..and replay old missions.&nbsp;

00:09:29.807 --> 00:09:33.360
Then, when you're ready -&nbsp;
you can Sortie onto your next mission.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:40.380
Except - I can't show you the next mission. I&nbsp;
actually can't show you the next four missions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:47.280
I can actually only show you 20 minutes of&nbsp;
footage from missions 1, 6, 7, 8, and 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:53.220
so please forgive me for all the screenshots&nbsp;
I've been showing you to save time here.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:58.620
Anyway - over the course of the next four&nbsp;
missions that I can't show you, I worked my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:05.280
way towards constructing an AC design that I call&nbsp;
"Black Flint". This build had gotten me through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:11.580
previous Armored Core games, so I figured it would&nbsp;
be able to get me through this game, as well.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:20.940
I love this build. It basically uses two sets&nbsp;
of missiles to break open enemy defenses...which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:25.147
often enables me to land direct hits&nbsp;
with the bazooka and assault rifle.

00:10:25.147 --> 00:10:25.229
But the biggest upside of this build was&nbsp;
that it was easy to pivot to better suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.229 --> 00:10:25.324
the mission. If an enemy was a large target that&nbsp;
needed to be stance broken, I could switch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:25.423
double bazookas and break their guard for days.
Or if the enemy had an agile moveset that demanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.423 --> 00:10:25.524
all of my attention - then I could switch to dual&nbsp;
assault rifles; mindlessly hold down the triggers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.524 --> 00:10:28.260
and just focus on dodging, instead.
This build was more than enough to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:33.120
me past the final boss we were&nbsp;
allowed to fight in mission 11.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.280
The end. But not really..Because simply&nbsp;
beating missions isn't really the endgame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:45.300
in Armored Core. The real test of your skill&nbsp;
is replaying those missions, and attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:52.773
achieve a perfect S rank in them. I felt like there was a chance I might unlock something cool if I S-ranked

00:10:52.773 --> 00:11:00.000
 a few misisons, so I gave it a shot.
To better my chances, I decided to quickly create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.700
a set of 4 diverse builds that I thought&nbsp;
might be better suited to each mission.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.800
So I made a tank build for mission 6.
A flying quadruped for mission 7.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:18.960
A lightweight melee build for mission 8.
And a heavy energy weapon biped for mission 11.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.060
Let me take you through each mission with&nbsp;
these builds. Starting...with mission 6.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.800
In true Armored Core fashion, each&nbsp;
mission starts with a briefing:

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:52.620
I can't show all of this..but basically: Balam is&nbsp;
one of the big corporations that wants to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:58.980
through the locals' defenses here on Rubicon.
And for this task, they've enlisted two of their

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:03.660
own AC's, as well as you. An independent mercenary.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:28.140
As I fight this enemy AC, I need to talk&nbsp;
about these tread legs I'm equipped with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:34.080
They completely change the way the game&nbsp;
plays - it feels more like driving around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:40.920
in a vehicle with these - and there's an incredible sense&nbsp;
of weight and momentum to everything...which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:46.380
comes in handy whenever you decide to&nbsp;
overboost charge into enemies, like this.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:54.180
What's more - tank and quad legs absorb&nbsp;
recoil from your weapons - meaning you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.120
can stay mobile while firing heavy weaponry.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.100
Then I destroyed the dam,&nbsp;I completed the mission,

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:09.960
and even though I thought I played&nbsp;
perfectly..I only received an A rank.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:16.080
So I attempted to S rank this mission one&nbsp;
more time - with this, a reverse-jointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:22.620
dual machine gunner. I danced circles around&nbsp;
my enemies, ignored the AC fight at the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.180
and simply focused on blowing up the&nbsp;
dam as fast as possible instead. 

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.300
With this, I finally received my S rank.

00:14:51.664 --> 00:14:57.000
So mission 6 was handed to you by&nbsp;
Balam Industries..but in mission 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:03.060
another corporation named "Arquebus" has&nbsp;
tasked you with taking on the STRIDER,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:08.700
a mobile resource base that has been extensively&nbsp;
weaponized by the Rubicon Liberation Front.

00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:15.780
To destroy this thing, you need to take out the&nbsp;
Eye, a giant laser cannon shielded by four main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:22.500
generators. These are your targets. That is,&nbsp;
if you can get close enough without the Eye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:24.700
incinerating you.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:36.540
The sheer scale of this enemy is absolutely&nbsp;
staggering. Previous games have had Arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:39.717
Forts like this before, but they've&nbsp;
never looked this good. 

00:15:55.080 --> 00:16:01.560
As you climb, you hear the Strider personnel&nbsp;
progressively get more panicked. They literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:08.140
try to detach parts of the Strider to try and&nbsp;
get rid of you. But it's no use.

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:23.820
In my S-rank attempt for this&nbsp;
mission, I made this energy weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.620
quadruped build. The unique thing about&nbsp;
quadrupeds in this game is that if you tap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:36.420
the jump button while airborne, you can hover&nbsp;
in place. It probably goes without saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:42.060
this was extremely useful in this mission..
Just for some perspective though, here's what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.829
I looked like on my first attempt.&nbsp;

00:16:51.333 --> 00:17:00.187
This goes on for a while. But even then, I still&nbsp;
somehow managed to destroy the Eye.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.840
With the strider down, Arquebus are&nbsp;
impressed with you, to say the least.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:34.620
This gets a foot in the door for your handler&nbsp;
to ask for a spot...in Operation Wallclimber.

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:43.680
Operation Wallclimber is epic in scale - not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:48.060
because you're taking out a large enemy or&nbsp;
anything...but because it's a long mission.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:56.940
First you have to find a way to get behind&nbsp;
two gatling cannons defending the city.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:00.780
Then you have to break through the&nbsp;
city itself...all while avoiding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:05.280
fire from the 16 snipers positioned on the wall.

00:18:14.254 --> 00:18:20.656
If you remember: in mission 6, the redguns were bragging that they'd be the first over the wall.

00:18:20.656 --> 00:18:29.481
But if you take the time to explore the city here, you'll find the wreck of Gun 4 Volta - your Redgun teammate, dead in the streets.

00:18:29.481 --> 00:18:36.360
I'm not sure what killed him, but the first real challenge&nbsp;for you 
is defeating the Tetrapod MT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:46.440
tucked away underneath the Wall's main gate.
These things are so much fun to fight. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:53.520
my build here excels at one-on-one combat, with&nbsp;
dual handguns and missiles to break the enemy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:59.040
guard, and a pile driver you have to pull out&nbsp;
of the weapon bay at just the right moment.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:08.760
And then after making your way all the way up&nbsp;and through 
the wall, it's time to face the final boss.

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.660
This boss is easy in enough in the&nbsp;
first phase when you have a co-op&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:42.720
partner. But it's extremely difficult in&nbsp;
the second phase, when your partner leaves.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.200
Without IV Rusty on my side, I failed my S rank attempt against this mission.

00:20:06.613 --> 00:20:10.980
But when you fail a mission in Armored&nbsp;
Core 6 - you don't always have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.400
start from the beginning. Usually&nbsp;
you'll have unlocked a checkpoint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:20.880
and can re-attempt the part where you died with&nbsp;
replenished health, healing, and ammunition.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:26.640
What's more, you're actually able to switch&nbsp;
up your assembly mid-mission as well.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:29.880
So when I failed in my first&nbsp;
playthrough on this boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:36.420
I simply switched to double bazookas, and kept&nbsp;
the juggernaut staggered almost indefinitely.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:44.777
Yeah...so checkpoints are a powerful tool.&nbsp;And honestly I think the game's difficulty is based around

00:20:44.777 --> 00:20:47.329
You needing to make use of checkpoints.

00:20:47.329 --> 00:20:57.126
 Checkpoints are what give From Software the design space to create long missions and difficult encounters within those missions. 

00:20:57.126 --> 00:21:03.069
But naturally if you die and use a checkpoint then you're not going to get an S rank for the mission.

00:21:03.069 --> 00:21:05.700
So when I failed my S rank attempt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:11.880
I simply switched to double bazookas again,&nbsp;
and overcame the mission that way, instead.

00:21:13.704 --> 00:21:16.680
Might as well finish the mission, and&nbsp;
claim those extra credits, right?

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:23.340
The last mission I can show is&nbsp;
mission 11. Another long one.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:38.820
The first part of the mission involves&nbsp;
fighting your way past two fields of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:43.080
enemies with long-ranged weaponry.

00:21:49.524 --> 00:21:58.049
Later there's a 1v1 fight against an&nbsp;enemy AC named Sulla - who clearly&nbsp;
has a history with your handler.

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:31.320
and finally, there's a boss battle - a bullet&nbsp;
hell against the autonomous craft named BALTEUS.

00:24:04.976 --> 00:24:08.100
This enemy has an insane moveset.&nbsp;

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:14.580
The way I overcame this on my first playthrough&nbsp;
was by pivoting to dual assault rifles so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:21.060
I could just mindlessly hold my fire button&nbsp;
downs and focus on predicting and dodging its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:30.840
attacks, instead. And that worked. Just.
For my S rank attempt, I created Samus - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:40.151
plasma weapon build with dual pulse guns, a laser&nbsp;
cannon, and a shield...and it was awful. Or maybe I was awful, I'm not sure.

00:24:40.151 --> 00:24:45.480
I had some really cool moments,&nbsp;
but between dodging, and flying, and using my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:45.480 --> 00:24:51.720
shield, and charging my cannon, and managing the&nbsp;
overheat on my pulse guns..piloting this build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.720 --> 00:25:05.264
was just way beyond me, and I got hit. A lot.
So yeah. Even with the checkpoint system, the game is tough..and while I'm proud of the S ranks I did achieve,

00:25:05.264 --> 00:25:11.640
I failed at achieving them&nbsp;
for the longer missions with the limited time that I had.

00:25:29.376 --> 00:25:37.860
So I've used up just about all of the unique footage I have to show you today. But I still have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:43.440
to reveal! If you want to stick around, I'd like&nbsp;
to go over a bunch of new game mechanics that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:50.220
haven't mentioned yet, and at the end I'll also&nbsp;
share some deeper thoughts on the game, along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:55.860
with some details I learned from an interview&nbsp;
with the game's lead producer, and director.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:04.140
So..do you remember when I fought an enemy AC,&nbsp;
back in mission 6? His name was Index Dunham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:08.760
and his was an optional AC fight&nbsp;
where you kind of just overwhelm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:10.710
him with two teammates.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:18.300
So this fight is actually optional. But it's still absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:26.760
worth doing - because - you see this little icon&nbsp;
here? That's a combat log marker. It's a resource&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.760 --> 00:26:32.760
for something called Loghunt, which rewards&nbsp;
you with new parts for handing in combat logs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:38.640
and rising through the ranks. Very cool.
At any rate, you can actually rematch Index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:45.600
Dunham..and a lot of other AC fights too&nbsp;
for that matter - in the Arena. In glorious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:52.260
simulated, 1v1 combat. Which..I don't think&nbsp;
I'm allowed to show you. All I can show are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:57.480
these menu screens from the garage..where&nbsp;
it reveals that you are rewarded for these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:03.120
fights with credits, seen here..but more&nbsp;
importantly; with OST chips, as well.

00:27:03.120 --> 00:27:11.220
OST stands for Operating System Tuning, which&nbsp;
is also accessible in the garage. And it's here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.220 --> 00:27:17.640
that you can spend your OS chips earned from&nbsp;
the arena across four different categories. 

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:23.700
First are system unlocks, which give you&nbsp;
new baseline abilities. Some of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:30.840
like Boost Kick and the Weapon Bay I'd argue are&nbsp;
almost essential. Boost Kick lets you press your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:37.440
assault boost button at the end to deal huge&nbsp;
impact damage to enemies, like this...and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:43.020
Weapon Bay lets you equip arm weapons to&nbsp;
your shoulders. Note that you can't fire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:48.960
these shouldered arm weapons at all times like&nbsp;
you can with regular shoulder weapons. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:57.780
you have to switch to them manually, like this. 
Then there's core expansions, which are special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:05.820
offensive or defensive abilities. I experimented&nbsp;
with Assault Armor, which does a huge AOE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:15.960
like this...I used Pulse Protection, which puts a&nbsp;
barrier in place like this...and I tried Terminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:23.100
Armor, which will keep you alive at 1AP in a&nbsp;
situation where you usually would have died.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:31.560
Finally, there are Attack Control and Damage&nbsp;
Control categories, which simply increase the % of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:31.560 --> 00:28:38.400
damage you deal, or the amount of damage you can&nbsp;
heal, or reduce a % of the damage you can take.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:47.220
So upgrading your OS is a way to straight up make&nbsp;
your AC a bit stronger, or a bit more versatile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:56.220
This is in contrast to the general customisation&nbsp;
process, where every part is balanced by being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.220 --> 00:29:02.280
strong in some ways, but weaker in others.
Next, I want to quickly talk about energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:07.680
weapons. Energy weapons don't work like&nbsp;
they did in previous games.

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:15.960
Back then they required a part of your actual energy bar to&nbsp;
fire, now their gimmick is that energy weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:23.100
will overheat if you fire them too quickly. So&nbsp;
they don't reload in the conventional sense..

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:30.480
Which means you have the option to either fire them until&nbsp;
they overheat, or to use them sparingly to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:36.300
prevent them from overheating. Additionally,&nbsp;
it seems energy weapons can also be used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:50.820
cancel out energy defenses of the same type. 
Finally, let's talk lock-on. Lock-on is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:57.060
automatic, meaning as soon as an enemy is close&nbsp;
enough and in your sights, your Fire Control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.060 --> 00:30:03.900
System will target them. As mentioned before,&nbsp;
there's a soft lock state where you have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:11.460
free movement, but there's also a hard-lock state&nbsp;
called "Target assist" where your character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:20.220
camera will perfectly follow the enemy. To my&nbsp;
knowledge, this hard lock cannot be broken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:28.080
and your AC will automatically quick turn&nbsp;
perfectly to face your hard-locked target.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:39.120
So...target assist is very powerful. However,&nbsp;
you might have noticed that a lot of the time I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:47.820
played with soft-lock and moved my camera manually&nbsp;
instead. Now: I was pretty comfortable playing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:53.340
this manner because I have played a lot of&nbsp;
previous Armored Core games, so it felt more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.340 --> 00:31:00.060
second-nature to me. And playing in this manner&nbsp;
DOES give you some benefits. Your motion is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:06.000
bit more free, and whichever enemy is closest&nbsp;
to the center of your screen will be targeted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:11.160
so you also have more control over who&nbsp;
you're firing at - which is especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.160 --> 00:31:17.220
useful when you're surrounded by enemies.
HOWEVER the downside of moving your camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:25.680
manually with the right stick is that it means&nbsp;
you don't have your thumb free to press the face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:33.210
buttons required for dodge, and ascend. And I&nbsp;
found I couldn't really claw grip to hit those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.210 --> 00:31:38.040
buttons either. Remember, your pointer&nbsp;
and middle fingers are on all four of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:46.140
weapon triggers. So...if you want to play soft&nbsp;
locked and control your camera with your thumb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.140 --> 00:31:52.440
it essentially means your thumb will have to&nbsp;
dart over and tap the buttons for dodge and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.440 --> 00:32:00.360
flight. Which - can be done, but you have to stop&nbsp;
controlling the camera for a moment to do so.

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:07.200
Therefore, I think most people will naturally&nbsp;
gravitate towards playing with target assist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:07.200 --> 00:32:16.740
turned on most of the time so that they&nbsp;
can be hard locked to enemies instead. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:23.760
this means giving up a bit of control and aim, but&nbsp;
it frees your thumb for dodging and flying, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:30.420
think those things are generally more important.
Speaking of aiming, let's finally talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:40.020
FCS part. The FCS part essentially determines&nbsp;
how accurate your aim is. See that little reticle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.020 --> 00:32:48.000
trailing the enemy within my lock box? That&nbsp;
reticle is not always on point. At certain ranges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:58.560
it will waver, or trail the enemy. 
So each FCS has three main variables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:05.220
Its close-range assist, its medium-range&nbsp;
assist, and its long range assist. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:11.760
these values define how good your FCS will be&nbsp;
when tracking enemies at these ranges - meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:22.260
you have to think very critically about what sort&nbsp;
of weapons you're wielding, and how close you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:29.280
be to enemies, before choosing your FCS part.
FCS also determine the time it takes your missiles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:37.080
to lock onto enemies, and also how long it takes&nbsp;
to acquire a multi-lock on multiple enemies.

00:33:37.080 --> 00:33:40.860
Finally, I'd like to give some more&nbsp;
personal thoughts on the game.
&nbsp;

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:48.060
First, the story: It's hard to get this point&nbsp;
across with limited footage, but as missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.060 --> 00:33:52.980
play out and you're slowly introduced&nbsp;
to all the characters and corporations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.960 --> 00:34:01.680
you really start to feel..small. Like you're&nbsp;
just some small piece of the puzzle within your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:08.160
handler's plans, who is himself a small&nbsp;
part of the larger corporation plans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:20.820
who are all a small part of this huge war over&nbsp;
a resource that is unfolding on Rubicon. I am in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:27.120
love with the vibe of this game..and the amazing&nbsp;
soundtrack is playing a huge in that as well. 
&nbsp;

00:34:27.120 --> 00:34:34.920
In terms of endings, the game is confirmed&nbsp;
to have multiple.. and from my interview with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:41.700
Director Yamamura-san and Lead producer Ogura-san,&nbsp;
it sounds like endings will work a lot like they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.700 --> 00:34:47.460
did in the fourth generation of Armored Core,&nbsp;
where new missions will be revealed in NG+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:52.500
and those new missions can be used to&nbsp;
take you towards different endings.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:16.620
Next let's talk gameplay. I could talk for an&nbsp;
hour on this point..but what I really want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:25.440
convey to you is how good the movement feels in&nbsp;
this game. And it all comes down to the leg types.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:33.840
When you're piloting a reverse joint, you feel&nbsp;
like your legs can really claw into the ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:40.800
and fling you off to the side, or high into the&nbsp;
air. When you throw on a pair of tank treads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:40.800 --> 00:35:51.780
you really feel like you're driving a heavy&nbsp;
vehicle with weight and momentum. Or if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.780 --> 00:36:03.180
want a feeling of weightlessness, then you&nbsp;
can opt for quad legs. I don't know of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:09.960
games that let you change the way your character&nbsp;
controls on such a fundamental level, and it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:31.920
so much fun playing with different builds.
Now...considering the recent success of Elden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:38.760
Ring, a big question on a lot of people's minds&nbsp;
is: will Elden Ring fans enjoy Armored Core 6?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.760 --> 00:36:45.360
And I think they will. Because..while&nbsp;
this is a very different genre of game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:52.200
it's also the first Armored Core to have a lot&nbsp;
of things in common with Souls. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.200 --> 00:37:00.300
Armored Core 6 now has a completely decoupled&nbsp;
camera. Your mech has fast turning speeds. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:07.320
can heal. There are checkpoints. Bosses have&nbsp;
heavily telegraphed attacks. There's a visual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:15.960
stagger system, a timed block system, and a&nbsp;
hard lock too. Yes, I know other games have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:38.880
these things. But Armored Core traditionally has&nbsp;
not. And I know a lot of people did not expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:38.880 --> 00:37:46.020
these things to be quite so present when Armored&nbsp;
Core 6 was first announced. But they are now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:50.040
and it absolutely makes the game&nbsp;
more accessible for Souls fans.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:58.740
What I'm really happy about is that it&nbsp;
still feels a lot like an Armored Core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:04.620
game even with all these things. Most&nbsp;
importantly, it's shaping up to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:11.820
be a good game with these things..and that's all&nbsp;
most people care about. This is just yet another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:18.180
familiar generational shift in the gameplay of&nbsp;
Armored Core..and I'm here for it, personally.

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:25.680
That said, I do still have some concerns about&nbsp;
the game. These aren't terrible problems or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:32.040
anything..it's more that they're things that I'm&nbsp;
not really sure how From Software will handle.

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:40.200
First and foremost: PvP. 1v1 PvP in Armored&nbsp;
Core has traditionally been about getting out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.200 --> 00:38:47.040
of your opponent's sights and outmaneuvering&nbsp;
them, but with the way target assist works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:47.040 --> 00:39:00.780
in Armored Core 6...I just don't see a way you&nbsp;
can outplay people in this manner anymore. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:07.140
I actually got the chance to ask Yamamura-san,&nbsp;
the game's director..about how skill expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.140 --> 00:39:13.920
in PvP would work with hard lock being a&nbsp;
thing..and he replied with a few insights:

00:39:13.920 --> 00:39:20.040
1) First, he stated that target assist&nbsp;
actually forces you to move and fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:27.840
with a certain angle of attack, so it's possible&nbsp;
soft lock could help with your evasion. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:33.780
mentioned that flying over the top of enemies&nbsp;
might help to prevent you getting hard locked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.184 --> 00:39:49.800
2) Additionally, he says you have to consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:57.540
enemy stagger meters, as well as your own. The&nbsp;
way you fire weapons to build up stagger and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:04.980
then make use of exposed defenses plays a role&nbsp;
in skill expression, and you often won't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:11.400
away with just firing all four weapons at once.
3) And then there's the need to maintain optimal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:17.400
distance, which plays a part in dealing&nbsp;
the right amount of damage. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.400 --> 00:40:24.660
both players will be fighting to maintain&nbsp;
that ideal distance at all times.

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:30.660
4) And then there's timing, of course, which&nbsp;
is also very important to dodge enemy fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:30.660 --> 00:40:38.160
and when to fire your attacks so that they land.
Lastly though - we've just learned that Armored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.160 --> 00:40:45.480
Core 6 will feature 6-player multiplayer. And&nbsp;
I will say: I think that target lock should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:51.466
be less impactful in 2v2 or 3v3 combat, as&nbsp;
those game modes would also require to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.466 --> 00:40:54.420
very aware of what's going on around them,&nbsp;
rather than just honing in on one player.

00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:02.520
But then another reason I'm curious PvP is..I&nbsp;
just think it's going to be extremely hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:09.180
for From Software to balance the fact that you&nbsp;
can fire four weapons at once, in this game.

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:15.840
When I asked Yamamura-san about the biggest&nbsp;
challenges they faced when developing Armored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:24.180
Core, he answered that balancing weapons was&nbsp;
a key focus for him and the team. And I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:32.340
see why! In a lot of games, naturally, there are&nbsp;
overpowered weapons. But remember, AC6 is unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:39.600
in that..you can fire four weapons at once. So if&nbsp;
you're firing four "overpowered" weapons at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:47.040
just think about how much that exacerbates any&nbsp;
power imbalance. And in this online age, naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:53.880
everyone is going to talk about and recommend&nbsp;
the use those overpowered weapons - especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.880 --> 00:42:02.160
in PvP. So From Software will absolutely have&nbsp;
their work cut out for them in balancing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:09.060
game after release. I think to be the best&nbsp;
experience possible, it's going to need more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:15.660
high quality balance patches than they usually&nbsp;
put out. But they're aware of this, I'm sure.

00:42:15.660 --> 00:42:27.180
In terms of difficulty, encounters seem&nbsp;
hard enough - don't worry about that. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:27.180 --> 00:42:34.200
limited footage naturally kind of shows my best&nbsp;
moments, but I struggled a good amount at times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:40.620
trust me. Checkpoints make the difficulty&nbsp;
less inconvenient - for sure..but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:55.920
what I kind of worry might be overpowered about&nbsp;
checkpoints is the ability to switch up your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:55.920 --> 00:43:07.800
mech's assembly halfway through a mission. As&nbsp;
a fan of the older games, I find think changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.960
your mech halfway is a bit immersion breaking&nbsp;
and I think it might also risk being a bit too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:12.960 --> 00:43:26.340
forgiving..but hey, maybe it's too early to say.
The last thing I want to talk about is debt. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:33.960
previous games I kind of loved the threat of&nbsp;
going into debt by playing badly and losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:33.960 --> 00:43:44.700
credits..but going into debt is not going to be a&nbsp;
thing in Armored Core 6. They've confirmed that. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:52.140
also want to note however, that AC6 is extremely&nbsp;
generous with the credits it gives you. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.140 --> 00:44:01.680
that, but it's very easy to replay missions,&nbsp;
so you are going to naturally have a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.680 --> 00:44:09.600
credits. And that's by design: when I asked him&nbsp;
about this, Yamamura-san basically said that From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:26.580
Software felt that too much debt might dampen&nbsp;
the fun of the assembly aspect. And I get that:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:31.980
The benefit to having more credits is that it&nbsp;
encourages you to buy parts, and experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:36.000
with things, and having more parts that you own&nbsp;
also allows you more freedom to switch up your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:40.620
assembly mid-mission, which, as mentioned before&nbsp;
- is clearly a big part of this game now.
&nbsp;

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:49.740
But do you know what else Yamamura-san said?&nbsp;
He admitted that he too was a big fan of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:54.900
debt you could accrue in previous games. And&nbsp;
that even though it was a punishing system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:54.900 --> 00:45:00.240
he said he liked the feeling it&nbsp;
gave; that sense of a severe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:04.440
oppressive mercenary lifestyle.
And so the last point I'll leave you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:10.620
on is what he said right afterwards. He said&nbsp;
that if they have some nice ideas in-house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:15.540
they might try to revisit the concept&nbsp;
of debt in some form in the future.
&nbsp;

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:25.140
So...Fires of Rubicon sequel..confirmed!? Probably&nbsp;
too early to say for sure..but I know employees of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:31.200
From Software have a soft spot in their hearts&nbsp;
for this franchise..and I really hope that Fires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:36.060
of Rubicon resonates with people so that they&nbsp;
can get the greenlight to make more of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:42.480
games. Especially if it means that Fromsoft will&nbsp;
start reintroducing some of the more hardcore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:50.751
more traditional elements..of Armored Core.

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:10.140
If you're curious about what those traditional&nbsp;elements are, consider watching my video&nbsp;
where I play through every single prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.140 --> 00:46:16.620
Armored Core game. Or come and ask me in real&nbsp;
time! I'm currently live on Twitch right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:22.380
attempting to answer everyone's questions&nbsp;
about my time with Armored Core 6.

00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:27.960
Other than that, thank you for listening to me&nbsp;
ramble..subscribe and be sure to switch on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:32.760
bell notification so you don't miss new Armored&nbsp;
Core videos..and I'll see you in the next one.

